---
import Layout from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import XiaoMaoStory from "../components/XiaoMaoStory.astro";
import { base } from "../lib/site";
---
<Layout
  title="小猫钓鱼 · what attention is for"
  description="A retelling of 小猫钓鱼, then an analogy (not a technical proof) about large language models, attention, and staying with a task."
  canonical="https://zhao-langxi.github.io/zhao-langxi/xiao-mao-diao-yu.html"
  current="xiaomao"
  bodyClass="story-page"
  ogTitle="小猫钓鱼 · The Little Cat Goes Fishing"
  ogDescription="A Chinese childhood story about focus, and what it can (and cannot) teach us about large language models."
>
  <main id="main" tabindex="-1">
    <header>
      <p>赵郎溪 · a picture-book lesson</p>
      <h1 lang="zh-Hans">小猫钓鱼</h1>
      <p>
        The Little Cat Goes Fishing. A story I keep coming back to, then a clearly marked analogy
        about large language models (LLMs). The story is the story. The comparison is not a lecture
        on how transformers work.
      </p>
    </header>

    <article>
      <XiaoMaoStory idPrefix="storypage" showFishing={true} />

      <section id="llm" aria-labelledby="llm-heading">
        <h2 id="llm-heading">The LLM connection</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Interpreted, as an analogy.</strong>
          This is not a literal explanation of transformer attention, and it is not a research paper.
          The childhood lesson is about giving one task your whole heart. Building reliable AI is
          about knowing what deserves attention.
        </p>
        <blockquote class="xiaomao__lesson">
          <p>
            小猫钓鱼 taught us the importance of paying attention. Building reliable AI teaches us
            the importance of knowing what deserves attention.
          </p>
        </blockquote>
        <table class="xiaomao__table">
          <caption>How I hold the two pictures side by side</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">In the story</th>
              <th scope="col">In an LLM</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>The kitten goes fishing</td>
              <td>The model receives a task or prompt</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Butterflies distract the kitten</td>
              <td>Irrelevant information sits in the same context</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>The kitten forgets the rod</td>
              <td>The answer can drift from the instruction</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Mother Cat asks for 一心一意</td>
              <td>Clear goals and checks help keep the work on the task</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>The kitten sits, and a fish comes</td>
              <td>The model returns something that actually helps</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <h3>Attention is not the same as understanding</h3>
        <p>
          Many modern LLMs are built on transformers. Those models use a mechanism also called
          attention: a way of weighting which pieces of the available text matter when producing
          the next piece. That is a calculation over tokens. It is not the kitten deciding to sit
          still. Weights do not guarantee that a system understands a person's goal.
        </p>

        <h3>Context can contain butterflies</h3>
        <p>
          If you ask for an explanation of Python loops and also pour in movies, restaurant hours,
          and a stack of unrelated notes, the model can still read the pile. The extra material can
          still pull the answer off course. Useful prompting is often just: keep what the task needs,
          leave the rest on the bank.
        </p>

        <h3>Agents wander too</h3>
        <p>
          An AI agent might search, draft, test, and revise. Like the kitten, it can take a step
          that does not move the original goal. Clear objectives, checkpoints, and a look back at
          the work are how you keep a line in the water.
        </p>
        <p>
          I am an Informatics student, not publishing a lab result here. If you want the hire-facing
          ships, they live on
          <a href="https://jadexzhao.github.io/jadexzhao/" rel="noopener noreferrer">jadexzhao</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <p>
        <a href={base}>← Home</a>
        <a href={`${base}about.html`}>About</a>
        <a href={`${base}archive.html`}>Older writing</a>
      </p>
    </article>
  </main>
</Layout>
